# Who has to review a change to the things IAM trusts.
#
# Write access to this repository is merge access to five workflow files pinned by name in
# three IAM trust policies -- and to the source and configuration that ship inside the two
# released Lambda zips. tools/build_admission_lambda.py copies config/*.yaml and the whole
# src/edullm_platform tree into the validator, so a change to either decides whether a run
# is authorized. That makes them the same kind of path as a workflow file rather than a
# different one, and they were not covered here.
#
# Extended when write access was first granted to somebody who did not build this platform.
# Until then, ownership covered the workflows and the infrastructure and left the admission
# validator's own source and the policy it enforces uncovered, so a change to the
# authorization rules needed one approving review from anybody holding write.
#
# CONFIG IS OWNED MORE WIDELY THAN THE REST, AND ONLY CONFIG. 84 of the last 99 commits on
# main touched a path owned here and 29 of them touched config, counted on 2026-08-04
# against the first-parent diff of each commit. Branch protection sets
# require_code_owner_reviews with required_approving_review_count 1, and all 36
# collaborators already hold write or better. So the review queue for eight research teams
# was two people, and the largest single thing they were queueing on was config: profiles,
# workloads, the roster, datasets.
#
# config/ is also the least dangerous place to be wrong. Every file under it is loaded and
# cross-checked by the suite, which catches an incoherent profile, an unplaceable workload
# or a roster naming somebody who is not in the organization inside ninety seconds -- so a
# bad config change is caught by a machine before a reviewer would reach it. A trust policy
# or a workflow file is not like that: the failure is a run that cannot assume a role, in
# an account no test here can see. Those stay with the two.
#
# THE EIGHT LOGINS ARE SPELLED OUT RATHER THAN NAMED AS @edu-llm/team-leads, on purpose.
# They are config/organization.yaml's `team_leads` list, and the comment at the top of that
# file records what happens when the two disagree: the GitHub team went two days out of
# step with the roster after the Memory group changed lead, and nothing in this repository
# could notice. The two agree today, login for login. Naming the team here would make this
# file silently follow the next disagreement; naming the people makes a change to who
# reviews config a change somebody commits.
